Screaming at the World
The marketing campaign Looks Like You Need Iceland is thought to have started well and shouts and calls in nature are starting to pull people all over the world.
“You’re got the mystery, I need Iceland,” a woman in the big world commented on an advertisement from the marketing campaign Looks Like You Need Iceland on YouTube.
Another observer says she will preserve Iceland in her heart for all eternity, and a young woman says she must get to Iceland as soon as possible, as her fiancé resides there.
Luckily, however, she's in the United States, so she expects to have to wait a few more months.
Another observer asks if the hiking trail to Iceland is capable, because there are no planes.
It doesn't follow the story whether it comes from a different time zone.
Egill Þórðarson from the advertising agency Peel, who was involved in the preparation of the advertisements along with the international advertising agency M&C Saatchi, says the reception has exceeded expectations.
“I’ve been involved in a lot of Inspired By Iceland campaigns that have been going very well, but this new campaign has already begun.
Basically, it's all about creating a PR value, it's getting foreign media to cover the campaign, and it's been incredibly successful.
In a relatively short period of time, we've received coverage in about seven hundred media outlets around the world covering a total of more than two billion people.
The value of that coverage is priced at 1.8 billion krónur.
It's not bad."
The main market is the United States, and the campaign has been going very well, even though Americans aren't on their way to the country anytime soon.
According to Egil, well-known markets such as Denmark, the United Kingdom and Germany have also been welcoming, such as Russia, which is a pleasant surprise.
Then came a reaction from exotic areas, such as India, which was not specifically proposed.
“These advertisements have travelled more widely than we expected,” says Egill. It’s worth noting that over four million people have watched the content on YouTube.
“We couldn’t have asked for a better start.”
The photos here at the opening were taken by Árni Sæberg, photographer of Morgunblaðið, in the making of advertisements entitled “Let It Out” in the middle of last month.
The filming took place widely, such as on Skólavörðustígur in Reykjavík, Reynisfjara beach, Skógarfoss waterfall, Sólheimajökull glacier and in a cave by Hjörleifshöfði.
The directors were Samúel Bjarki Pétursson and Gunnar Páll Ólafsson of Skoti productions and cameraman Óttar Guðnason.
The roles in the series included the Icelandic Anna Jia and Murphy Cardenas, who is from Cuba and Hungary.
Dozens of Icelanders were involved in the preparation of the advertisements, but at the same time as recordings took place in South Iceland, there was another group in the West and the West Fjords.
According to Egill, “Let It Out” is only the first part of the Looks Like You Need Iceland campaign, but the aim is a winter campaign in collaboration with M&C Saatchi.
“It’s just the first phase of this work for Promote Iceland,” says Egill, adding that the collaboration with M&C Saatchi has been extremely successful.
"It's great to work with them.
It’s very important to have foreign partners with knowledge of the markets we’re talking to.”
Sprengisandur: Discuss the situation in the labour market, pension funds and much more
Ásmundur Einar Daðason, Minister of Social Affairs and Children, arrives in Sprengisandur, which starts at ten o'clock in Bylgjan today.
He is also the Minister of Labour and discusses the situation in the labour market as such, the Icelandair affair and other issues.
He also discusses rural affairs and the relocation of jobs abroad, which has gone poorly and has been controversial in recent years and decades.
Ragnar Þór Ingólfsson, chairman of VR, will also attend the show. He will discuss his critiques regarding pension funds' investments and proposes that the labour movement and employers withdraw from the boards of the funds to reduce the risk of conflicts of interest.
Ívar Ingimarsson, a tourist in East Iceland, and Árnheiður Jóhannsdóttir, managing director of the North Iceland Marketing Office, will also be interviewed about the situation in tourism in the countryside.
They will look forward to the fall and wonder if the success of the summer is a misleading premonition of what is to come.
You can listen to the show below, but it starts at ten.
That's what it looks like today 25 years later
Actress Elisa Donovan had a successful career in teen shows of all kinds.
However, she gained worldwide fame for her role as Amber in Clueless 25 years ago.
She also starred in Sabrina: The Teenage Witch alongside Melissa Joan Hart, A Night at the Roxbury and Beverly Hills 90210.
Donovan is now 49 years old, married with one child.
Little has been said about it lately, but she managed to look back and talk about Clueless on Australian television recently.
She speaks beautifully about Brittany Murphy, who died unexpectedly in 2009 and says she was a great person.
“I have to admit that I built the character mostly on girls I met at a school that wasn’t good to me.
As soon as I read the script, I knew who that character was," Donovan said of his performance in Clueless.
Didn't want to leave the police station after an overnight stay
About fifty cases came to the table of the police in the capital area today, and the police's diary states that the cases were diverse.
The day at the police station in Hverfisgata began with police having to intervene with a man who had then recently been released from a prison cell after having stayed there due to drunk driving.
Didn't the man want to go away after he was released and didn't obey the instructions of the police who told him to leave.
The man earned a continued stay at the police station because of his conduct.
A man was arrested in the morning on suspicion of burglary at a company in Kópavogur.
Police also had to intervene with two men in Breiðholt due to reports of assault.
It also happened that a woman who was having dinner at a shopping centre in Kópavogur could not pay for the bill after the meal and was therefore called to the police.
There were also reports of the theft of a car in the city centre this morning.
The driver of a white Renault van searched from her for a short time and she was then taken unfree.
The car hasn't been found.
Curses of the Gleaser - Terrible Fate of the Glee Stars
The theory has made flight on the Internet that a curse rests over the television series about the Gleaning Ensemble, but actress Naya Rivera drowned in California recently, becoming the show's third lead actor to die far in advance.
Glee is a popular series about singing, dancing, and joy.
The series covered the so-called teenage ensemble in high school, their love and destiny.
It wasn't always taken out with pleasure to be in the joy band, but through singing and dancing, the show's characters seemed to be able to overcome whatever circumstances arose in their lives.
But outside of the set, there has been a great catastrophe over the actors and employees of the show, so much so that theories have arisen that a curse rests on the show.
Naya Marie Rivera took the role of Santana Lopez on the show, a cheerleader who didn't call everything her grandmother.
After the season's debut, Rivera married actor Ryan Dorsey and had with him his first and online child, Josey.
In 2017, Rivera was arrested for domestic violence against her husband and subsequently divorced them.
Dorsey, however, declined to file charges and the domestic violence case was dropped.
On July 8, it was reported that Riveru was missing after her four-year-old son was found alone drifting in a boat on Lake Piru, California.
The boy was found asleep in a lifejacket aboard the boat and was able to inform the police that he and his mother had stabbed himself for a swim, and that Rivera had brought him back into the boat but had never returned there herself.
An extensive search for Rivera began.
The day after the search began, the police chief in the area reported that during the search it was assumed that Rivera had drowned.
Five days later Rivera was found and officially pronounced dead.
It is believed to have encountered heavy currents in the water and made use of her last claim to save the son.
The cause of death was recorded drunk by accident.
Cory Allan Michael Monteith took on the role of Finn Hudson on the show, the American footballer with the angel voice who played a key role in bringing the band to glory.
However, Moneith struggled with personal demons.
From the age of 13, he had struggled with addiction and found it difficult to escape it.
In 2013, his co-workers on the show felt enough, intervened and urged him to seek help.
Moneith then underwent treatment and everything seemed to look better.
Just two months after completing his treatment, Moneith was found dead in a hotel room after going out having fun with friends.
Banamein was found to be a deadly combination of drugs and alcohol.
It wasn't believed to be intentional.
Moneith had undergone treatment and his tolerance to intoxicants had declined so much that a dose he had previously tolerated well, proved fatal.
He was just 31 when he was killed.
His death weighed heavily on his colleagues, but at the insistence of his counterpart Lea Michele, it was decided to continue filming and they devoted an entire part to Moneith's memory and Finn's character.
Mark Wayne Salling played the role of Noah “Puck” Puckerman on the show, Puck was an American football player, like Finn, and had little respect for his fellow musicians in the Pleasure Squadron until he gained the courage to admit that he enjoyed singing and dancing.
Two years later, Salling was arrested at his home in Los Angeles on suspicion of possessing child pornography.
During a house search at his home, an enormous amount of child pornography was found, and the case was soon heard in public.
It was clear that Salling was done.
He was subsequently charged and convicted of his offences.
He faced four to seven years in prison, as well as having to be on the list of sex offenders and seek treatment for pedophile.
Before the judge determined the punishment, Salling, who was then released on bail, was found dead near his home.
The cause of death was suicide.
It wasn't just the show's actors who falled from far in advance.
Jim Fuller was the show's assistant director.
He died in his sleep in 2013 at the age of 41.
There was also a woman named Nancy Motes.
She was the younger sister of superstar Julia Roberts and didn't tell her beautiful story, accusing Roberts of great manipulation and degrading behavior.
Motes took her own life in 2014 but she struggled with severe depression.
She left a letter to her fiancé in which she said that her mother and sister were, among other things, responsible for how she was placed.
“My mother and my so-called siblings get nothing from me except the recollection that it was those who caused my worst depression.”
Lea Michel was the star of the show.
She has recently been accused of sexually assaulting her accompanists and aggression on the set.
Jesse Luken made guest appearances on the show in 2012.
He was arrested and charged with drunk driving in 2019, which was considered a major scandal.
Actress Heather Morris played an important role on the show.
In 2010, she discovered that unscrupulous hackers made nude photos of her and appeared on the Internet.
Actors Melissa Benoist and Blake Jenner both starred on the show and had an affair outside the show.
Benoist later revealed that Jenner abused her in the relationship.
You can put flowers on almost everything
It increases the development of children when they get to play outside in a garden with their parents to grow flowers and get to watch them grow and thrive.
It is also endlessly possible to do with flowers as the Swedish photographer Anna Kubel suggests.
Just spending time with the kids in the park is something that will never be forgotten.
They will remember the flowers, the aroma, and, of course, the precious times they had with their parents.
Bought a boat after 10th grade
“I don’t think we’re many fishermen who work in the country’s preschools,” says Axel Örn Guðmundsson, who engages in coastal fishing during the summer but studies psychology at the University of Iceland during the winter.”
Coastal fishing is a very convenient summer job when you are studying.
If you fish well, you can have a good income and I think it's great to be able to avoid taking student loans.
The income is also good for me during the winter,” says Axel Örn Guðmundsson, a 25-year-old psychology student, who is engaged in coastal fishing this summer as in recent summers.
Axel was most recent to dock in Tálknafjörður when a journalist spoke of him late in the evening at the beginning of the week.”
“The winter that I was in tenth grade, I took a captain’s license for boats that are less than twelve meters long and I bought my boat the summer after I finished primary school.
I've been fishing in my boat ever since.
I bought the boat used by my acquaintance, Hartmann Jónsson, and I gave the boat his name.
Hartmann had become an adult when I bought the boat and stopped being at sea and he was very happy when he saw that the boat bore his name.
Hartmann died a few years after I took over the boat," Axel said, adding that he bought the boat for three million.
“I made an agreement with Hartmann to pay half, one and a half million, at the beginning of the summer and the other half by the end of the summer when I had finished fishing.
“I had the boat free at the end of my first summer.”
But how could a boy have one and a half a million dollars to pay into a boat when he had just finished primary school?
“I had already paid and collected money, I had been working with my dad at sea when I was a boy, I spent all summers fishing with him and got my share.
“I also put my confirmation money into my boat fund.”
Axel was born in Ísafjörður, from where all his mother's people are, but he has lived in Kópavogur since he was a boy.
“I’m able to fish here in the western area because I’m domiciled with my grandma in Ísafjörður.
I studied these areas in the west of paddlers with my dad.
I move between the fjords depending on how I feel, because this fishing area covers the whole of the Westfjords.
Even though I think it's best to be in the west, I've also moved around Snæfellsnes peninsula and beyond.
“I’ve also hunted in the south,” says Axel, who always rows one early in the morning and says that he sometimes talks to the seagull and sings to the sky, in the solitude for hours in the ocean.
“The internet connection at sea is good, so I can call, listen to podcasts and music.
There’s no point in being alone.
Of course, I try to avoid getting into danger, but sometimes there has been something wrong with the weather, but there has never been much danger,” says Axel, who is lucky with the fact that he is never seasick.
He says the length of the working day depends on how the fishing goes at any given time.
“In coastal fishing, I’m never more than 14 hours fishing at a time, but I’ve also been fishing in other systems, for example in the rental quota, when I’ve spent one and a half a day grazing at sea fishing.”
The arrangement in coastal fishing, he says, is that he is allowed to catch 770 kilograms a day, which he finds restrictive.
“Other constraints are that I am not allowed to catch Fridays, Saturdays and Sundays, and I am only allowed to fish twelve days a month, during the four months of coastal fishing, in May, June, July and August.
In my opinion, fishermen should choose their fishing days depending on the weather and nothing else.
There is this unnecessary pressure on people instead of having 48 days throughout the summer and being able to choose according to their head.
In addition, the regional division could also go because of me, and the marine spaces that are well positioned by fishing grounds should enjoy it,” says Axel, who mostly extracts cod from salty sea during his coastal fishing, but also only saithe and occasional other types of rivers.
Axel works during the winter with his university studies at the kindergarten Núpur in Kópavogur.
“I don’t think we’re many fishermen who work in the country’s pre-schools,” Axel says proudly, adding that he only intended to work temporarily at the nursery.
“I’m stuck there because I think it’s an awesome job.
“I hope my psychology studies will be useful to me at the preschool level in the future,” says Axel, who has also come a long way with his studies in business administration.
Error in registration prevented homecoming precautions
Errors in a registration form that people fill out when they arrive in Iceland caused an individual, living in Iceland, not to be called back for testing.
It is not necessary to register your social security number if you fill out the form in English, as is when it is done in Icelandic.
"This will be examined this week," said an expert at the Department of Epidemiology.
Three domestic infections were diagnosed at the virology department of Landspítali yesterday.
One of those who tested positive arrived in the country on 15 July, two days after the rules on so-called homecoming precautions came into force.
Those who lived in Iceland are then screened on arrival in Iceland and are again summoned for testing four to five days later.
According to RÚV's lunchtime news, however, the person in question had complied with the old system.
The sampling at the border was negative and he was not summoned again for screening for the virus.
Kamilla Rut Sigfúsdóttir, a specialist at the Department of Epidemiology at the Directorate of Health, told the news agency that this can be traced to an error in the electronic registration sheet.
If people fill out the registration form in English, it is not a requirement to put in a social security number as in the Icelandic version.
First and foremost, it was intended for foreign tourists.
Therefore, the person in question has not received an automatic invitation to come for sampling.
Kamilla says that many people, however, have adhered to rules on a repatriation portal even though they have filled the registration form in English and in some cases, employers have also been vigilant to send people for testing again after they arrived in the country.
“We’ll look at how to make it easier for people to get their attention on the process.”
The man is in isolation and six have been quarantined.
They all go for testing but two were starting to show symptoms.
Infection tracking is now underway with the three infections that were detected yesterday, but it has mostly been completed due to two infections reported on Friday.
In both cases, the sequencing of deCODE genetics has revealed that this is a variant of the coronavirus that has not been found here before.
In the second case, the ties have focused on Israel, although it is known that anyone who brought the virus to the country had also traveled to other European countries.
Kamilla says it should be known tomorrow where the other virus was coming from.
A total of 15 are currently in isolation according to the website COVID. is.
135 are in quarantine.
Four out of five infections unconnected
Five domestic infections have been detected in Iceland in recent days.
Of these, four are completely unconnected and, for example, have not found the origin of the infection that arose at the Rey Cup sports tournament yesterday.
Infection tracking is still ongoing.
Originally over a dozen people were sent into quarantine but they were reduced to sixteen.
A total of 34 are in quarantine due to these new infections detected yesterday.
This is the second time in a short time that a sporting event has been detected and has raised questions about whether such events should be held.
Jóhann K. Jóhannsson, Director of Communications at the Department of Civil Protection, says that this is possible, as long as people follow guidelines and rules.
“We are constantly pushing all those who live here that individual infection control measures need to be maintained.
We also need to urge people who are holding events that there are definite rules going on that needs to be complied with.
An event can then be held," Johann said.
He says infection can be prevented by airing individual infection control measures.
Rules and guidelines are also under constant review.
“What needs to be done, and what the Department of Civil Protection and the Directorate of Health is constantly pressing for people, is to pay attention to these individual infection prevention measures.
Both at home and also with service companies.
People wash their hands and use alcohol.
It prevents infection from going forward," Johann said.
“We need to urge people to move forward on the good journey we were on, in order to keep up the progress we’ve made so far.”
40% of victims of COVID-19 had type 2 diabetes
Devon Brumfield heard through the phone how difficult it was for her father to catch his breath.
Her father had diabetes, so she urged him to seek medical attention.
The next day he was dead.
The death was traced to sudden breathing difficulties due to coronavirus infection.
Diabetes was indicated on the death certificate as an underlying problem and Brumfield, who also has diabetes, is terrified that the same will happen.
The Reuters news agency said Brumfield's fears were unfounded.
The figures from a new study commissioned by U.S. authorities show that nearly 40% of those who died had type 2 diabetes as an underlying disease.
When looking at the percentage of those who had not reached the age of 65, the ratio rises to half.
A U.S. study (CDC) involved more than 10,000 people in 15 countries that died from the coronavirus between February and May.
Jonathan Wortham, an infectious disease specialist at the CDC, says the results are striking, not least for those who have been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes and their loved ones.
A slow-moving survey was carried out, pointing the answers from the 12 countries that responded to comparable rates.
10 states, including California, Arizona and Colombia, aren't yet beginning to report underlying conditions.
"Diabetes was already an epidemic in slow growth.
Right now, COVID—19 has broken through like a powerful age,” reports Elbert Huang, director of the Center for Chronic Disease Research and Policy at the University of Chicago.
Diabetes is more common among blacks and people of Latin American descent, which has also fared worse from the coronavirus.
One of the best defenses for those with type 2 diabetes is to keep the disease under control with exercise, a healthy diet, and the assistance of a healthcare professional.
However, the coronavirus pandemic has made it difficult for many to maintain a routine.
In addition, the high price of insulin has forced some to continue to show up for work and thereby stop being exposed to the virus.
Reuters points out that U.S. authorities may have been aware of the dangers that those with type 2 diabetes could be caused by the virus.
When SARS coronavirus struck in 2003, more than 20% of them had the disease, and during the swine flu pandemic in 2009, this group had three times the risk of being hospitalized.
When MERS did so in 2012, one study showed that 60% of those who died or were hospitalized had diabetes.
Charles Dela Cruz, researcher at Yale University, says that because the impact of the COVID-19 virus could last longer, the pandemic has unveiled a number of previously naughty complications.
"I'm afraid we'll see a tsunami of problems when it's over," Andrew Bolton, chairman of the Diabetic International Association of Diabetics.
Physicians have warned that the coronavirus pandemic may indirectly lead to an increase in complications related to diabetes, e.g. kidney disease and kidney dialysis.
Similarly, new research indicates that coronavirus may lead to the proliferation of diabetes cases.
Reuters says scientists are trying to understand the link between the coronavirus and type 2 diabetes.
The virus attacks the heart, lungs and kidneys, organs that are already weaken in many people who have diabetes.
Then a high rate of glucose and lipide in diabetics can produce a true “cytokine storm”, as it is called when the immune system reacts too aggressively and attacks the body.
Damaged epithelial cells can also lead to inflammatory formation which in turn can cause deadly blood clots.
"It's all one big jigsaw puzzle," Dela Cruz said.
“It’s all related internally.”
The news has been corrected.
“We were offered champagne, then they left the room.”
The couple Ásrún Magnúsdóttir and Atli Bollason accepted an unusual request from the artist Ragnar Kjartansson to have sex in front of a camera for a work that the artist was setting up in Paris.
"We loved each other so it wasn't complicated."
Atli Bollason never forgets when he met his wife and mother, Ásrún Magnúsdóttir, for the first time.
“I was very impressed with it.
Of course, it was just the radiance, but I also thought it was incredibly cute.
"And again," he says.
Ásrún also remembers this, as the husband recalls the first meeting regularly.
"I've always heard this story.
“Last just this weekend,” she said, who was also attracted to her husband.
“I think there’s so much excitement around me, which I like.
There's a lot going on and a lot going on, and I'm fascinated by it."
Atli says that the couple have emphasized in their relationship to go their own ways.
“We don’t tie up the same knots as our companions.
“We’re afraid of the routines we’re talking about.”
That is possibly why they took the talk from visual artist Ragnar Kjartansson, even though it was mildly unusual.
“Kristín Anna our mutual friend contacts us and asks if we can come and have a dessert with herself, Ragga and Ragnar’s wife, on Snaps.”
They accepted the invitation, met the trio at Snaps and had dessert wine and separate desserts.
They listened to Ragnar who guided them through his plans for an art exhibition he planned to set up at the Palais de Tokyo in Paris in the autumn.
“He had a multi-channel video piece called Scenes from Western culture.
“He described it in a simple way.”
Ásrún says that he described the work as banal and decadent scenes from the everyday life of Westerners.
"He had sketches of all the scenes he was going to shoot and last time he told us what he wanted to know if we would be interested in participating."
The scene that Ragnar asked the couple to take part in was supposed to show young middle-class couples to make love in a minimalist room.
"It's intercourse with the beginning and end," Atli says.
The couple didn’t know Ragnar much even though they knew about him and him from them “but they knew us and thought we fit into it.
They said they didn't want to advertise for people.
Maybe they didn’t think they’d get the right people, they’re going to get the right people, they’re going to masturbate from performing for others.”
They greeted Ragnar and agreed to think about it, but it didn't take long.
“When we walked off Snaps, we said, ‘Don’t we just come into it?’
It was just like that.
“I trust Ragnar as an artist and his entire team,” says Ásrún and Atli.
“It’s about love and intimacy.”
The scene was filmed in a nice apartment on Mýrargata.
There was a very small film team; cameraman, Ragnar and the soundman and the couple.
After a recording had been turned on, everyone left the room to wait outside in the car except Atli and Ásrún.
“We didn’t decide what we should be.
We got some little guidance but we tried to forget the time and place.
Being unaware of what we're doing and for whom.
“There wasn’t anything played, we just loved each other, so it wasn’t complicated,” Ásrún says.
But was it romantic?
"Yes, it was a bit romantic," Atli and Ásrún agree.
“We were offered champagne before they left the room.
"It was a bit like being on holiday in a nice hotel in Paris."
The team was happy with the couple's performance in the work that moved them.
"When Tommi the cameraman looked at it, he cried, he thought it was so beautiful," Atli says.
“It’s just as much about love and intimacy as it does itself,” says Ásrún.
The couple's parents have seen the work and tells Atli that his mother heard the voice of his son at the Reykjavík Art Museum and then realized that he was part of the work.
"Then she turns around and starts thinking about it," he said.
"My mom and dad saw it in Paris.
We hadn’t told anyone like that like that, but then my dad sends me a message and says, “It’s nice to run into the little family at the Palais de Tokyo,” Ásrún recalls.
And do you think the participation in the project has deepened the relationship between Ásrún and Atli?
"We're still together, so maybe it'll deepen something.
At least this deepened my relationship with my family-in-law,” Ásrún laughs.
Ásrún herself saw the work in Copenhagen with her colleague.
"I thought it was a bit difficult not to be with you but with someone else," she says, turning to her husband.
“But it’s nice to see it because it’s just one scene in a much bigger piece and when you saw it with the other pieces it’s really cool.
I couldn’t get away from it and I was just proud.
And that’s where the little bean was in my stomach,” says Ásrún, who was pregnant with the couple’s second child when the scene was filmed.
“It was just beautiful.”
Anna Marsibil Clausen interviewed Ásrún and Atli in Love Stories on Channel 1.
Swansea with victory in previous semi-finals
Swansea won 1–0 at Brentford in a dramatic match.
This was the team's first game in the semi-finals of the play-off to qualify for the Premier League.
The match took place at Swansea's home stadium in Wales.
The score at half-time was 0-0.
Swansea's home fans conceded a penalty in the 64th minute but Andre Ayew burst the arc out of the penalty spot and let the defence go.
Only two minutes later, Brentford's player Rico Henry received a red card.
Brentford therefore played the rest of the game fewer and in the 82nd minute Swansea's players took advantage of the team difference.
Andre Ayew then compensated by scoring a superb goal and secured Swansea a 1–0 win.
Swansea lead 1–0 in the duel, but the second game takes place next Wednesday at Brentford's home ground.
An ambassador for five years since 2009
Jeffrey Gunter Ross, the U.S. ambassador to Iceland, is in the spotlight by the media after CBS reported this morning that he wanted an armed bodyguard where he feared for his life.
However, it has not gone smoothly for the President of the United States to appoint an ambassador in this country.
Since Carol Van Voorst resigned as U.S. ambassador in April 2009, a total of 62 months, more than five years.
Van Voorst left office under peculiar circumstances, but Kastljós revealed in 2009 that she should have received the Order of the Falcon.
On her way to a farewell meeting with the President of Iceland, she received a call from the President's Office stating that she would not be honoured.
After she left Iceland, she taught international relations at Army War College.
Sixteen months passed until Van Voorst's successor came to work, in September 2010.
Robert S. Connan had been appointeed as an ambassador, but then dropped out.
Loius Ariega had been working in the Foreign Service for decades.
He resigned in the autumn of 2013 and took over as ambassador to Guatemala.
Then we had another ambassador-free era.
Robert Barber was approved by the U.S. Congress in January 2015 and came to work shortly afterwards, when no ambassador had been present for 13 months.
Barber was politically appointed but not a diplomat, but had worked as a lawyer and supported Barack Obama's campaign fund.
He resigned as soon as Donald Trump took over the presidency on January 20, 2017, as is customary with a politically appointed ambassador.
Trump's administration spent an unusually long time appointing both ambassadors and high-ranking officials in Washington's political system.
It was two years before the U.S. Congress summoned Jeffrey Ross Gunter to a meeting to ask him and confirm his appointment as ambassador to Iceland.
In his testimony in parliament, he said that he had never been to Iceland, but often to Western Europe, but his wife, who has died, had been of Dutch origin.
Gunter is politically appointeed, was formerly a dermatologist in California and has been a prominent member of the Jewish Republican Jewish Coalition.
Sheldon Adelson founded it, but Adelson is an enthusiastic supporter of Donald Trump.
Gunter came to work in Iceland in May 2019, when there had been no ambassador in the country since the beginning of 2017, for two years and four months, which is the longest in Iceland.
The reason is both how long the Trump administration was nominating people for key positions as well as that in recent years the work of the U.S. Congress has dragged greatly, including confirming an ambassador to office.
Since 2009, there has been no ambassador in Iceland for more than five years.
However, this has not prevented construction on behalf of the Embassy, which recently opened its headquarters at Engjateig.
The construction is believed to have cost about 6.5 billion, but node-thick safety walls surround the building, and there is bulletproof glass in all the windows.
However, that doesn't seem to have been enough to fill the current ambassador's sense of security because he's said he'll fear for his life and has asked for an armed bodyguard.
Former ÍBV player suffered racism in Iceland - "It was a mistake to come to Iceland"
Former ÍBV player Tonny Mawejje claims to have been subjected to racism when he was in Iceland and that he regrets coming to Iceland.
This is according to an interview with Tonny that appeared on the Ugandan newspaper Daily Monitor.
Tonny recently joined Uganda Police FC, which plays in Uganda's top division.
In the interview with the Daily Monitor, Tonny discusses many things, including his time in Iceland, but he played with ÍBV, Val and Þrotti in Iceland.
"When I came to Iceland, I didn't play in midfield like I used to play.
"The captain of the team was in that position, but he also had the jersey number that I wanted to have, so I didn't get any of what I wanted," Tonny said.
Among the things Tonny discusses is the racism he suffered in Iceland.
He says this is a problem that many black players face when playing in Europe.
"It happened to me once, but since I didn't understand the language, I just ignored what was said to me.
Later, I heard about it, and then I asked my friend what it's about.
“He then told me that my opponent made racist remarks about me after I tackled him.”
In 2014, Tonny went from Iceland to Norway, where he joined Haugesund.
He said he made the mistakes he regretted the most.
Tonny had not been able to qualify for Haugesund's starting line-up, but he wanted to play more to reach the national team.
Then he asked to go back to Iceland on loan, but he then joined Val.
"It was a mistake to come back to Iceland on loan.
"I think if I'd stayed longer in Norway, I'd've got the chance I'd've had the chance," Tonny said, but he hoped that if he played well in Norway, he'd probably go further.
He says that the loan to Iceland has ruined his dreams of getting into a major team in Europe.
Found worm in a woman's throat
Physicians at St. Luke's International Hospital in Tokyo found a 3.8 cm long black worm in a woman's tonsil.
Doctors managed to pull the worm out with tongs.
A study found that a parasite was involved.
CNN reports about it.
The woman had a few days earlier eaten sashimi, which are thin meat slices.
The woman improved quickly after the worm was removed, but parasites such as these are often contained in raw meat or fish.
Sushi has increased, according to CNN, according to CNN.
Fearing that a second wave is about to begin in Europe
Spain is currently the focus of concerns about Europe's second coronavirus wave, where governments have taken steps to restrain the spread of the coronavirus pandemic.
In Catalonia, all entertainment life has been put on ice for two weeks, but there are more cities than Barcelona where infections are increasing.
Other European countries have also taken measures to increase the number of infections in Spain, but in the UK everyone has to quarantine upon returning from Spain, as in Norway, and the French have been warned against travelling to Spain.
Infections are also increasing again in France and Germany, where governments are trying to find a balance to curb the spread of the virus and restart the economy.
The situation in Europe, however, is good compared to the rest of the world, but the incidence of infection globally is approaching 300 thousand per day, which includes most infections in America and South Asia.
According to John Hopkins University, there are 16 million confirmed infections globally, and the number of deaths confirmed to be linked to the virus is 644 thousand.
Gerður ́s mosaics will finally enjoy itself to the fullest
In front of the Reykjavík Customs House is a large and deep hole.
There are men wearing orange helmets.
There's also a large grave.
And more smaller heavy machinery.
The street is closed to traffic.
But the pavement is capable, and there, many people now stop and look at artworks from millions of mosaic tiles—as if they had never seened it before.
Maybe they’ve never seen it before.
At least don't respect that.
The work has so far been somewhat hidden, directly in front of it there were parking spaces that were always besieged.
People parked there, locked the car in a hurry, and then jumped on their errands in the city center.
“Lively and diverse public spaces” and “attractive urban atmosphere” are the guiding lights for the reconstruction of Tryggvagata that is currently underway.
The aim is to beautify the area and allow the mosaics of Gerður Helgadóttir at Tollhúsið to enjoy the view better.
In front of the work there will be a square and since the area lies well next to the sun, it is considered suitable as a residence area for road users.
The artwork will be illuminated and the material will now enjoy itself better than before on this 142 square meter surface.
In the area there will also be small "fog sprayers", a kind of water sculptures, which offer play and provide a certain mystery to the area.
In this way, the renovations are described by the City of Reykjavík, which is responsible for construction along with Veitur Utilities.
Layers of water supply, district heating and electricity supply will be renewed.
Many of them have come of age, but the sewage and cold water pipes are from 1925 and have therefore served residents and businesses in the city centre for almost a century.
Once the street is reopened after construction has been completed, cars can drive around it again.
But it will, however, be a one-way street and at the same time create a quieter and more accessible space for walking.
According to information about the Customs House on the website of the Directorate of Customs, it is said that the building was taken into use in 1971, but its architect was Gísli Halldórsson.
Due to the fact that a portal warehouse passed through the house, a 250-square-foot windowless wall surface was formed into the street.
The building committee and the architect agreed that such a surface would adversely affect the overall streetscape, if no special measures were taken to adorn the appearance of the building.
Therefore, the parties agreed to have a permanent work of art installed there.
During this time there was a lot of talk about Gerður Helgadóttir, an artist, according to the summary.
She had worked extensively on mosaic works in Germany and elsewhere.
It was decided to contact her first before deciding whether a competition for the work would be held.
It had often been discussed that the work had to mirror life by the harbour, as the harbour had been the lifeblood of Reykjavík since it was made.
When the artist was interviewed, she became obsessed with such a work.
An agreement was reached that she would receive drawings and other assistance before leaving the country again, where she would work on the proposals abroad.
Gerður got the amount of time she herself decided it was necessary, and when she returned home she submitted a few suggestions for discussion.
It was immediately agreed to ask her to do the work.
At the same time, it was requested to enter into a collective agreement with her and the famous art company in Germany, the Brothers Oidtmann. Gerður had long worked with them on the installation of famous works of art in many parts of Europe.
Contracts were reached and Gerður prepared the artwork for the installation of the brothers' workshops, which were then responsible for the installation of the Customs House.
All the work was exceptionally well done, on behalf of both Gerður Helgadóttir and the Oidtmann brothers, according to the summary.
It has since withstood harsh Icelandic weather.
It took Gerði about two years to do the work, which was carried out and staged in 1972 and 1973.
The artist died two years after the completion of the Tollhús work at the age of 47.
Preparing the transfer of more institutions to the country
The Minister of Social Affairs, Ásmundur Einar Daðason, announces that more public institutions will be exported to Iceland in the near future.
That's what the minister said on the show Sprengisandur on Bylgjan for lunch.
