All the main things from the second day of the Championship
The 94th Icelandic Athletics Championship ended at Þórsvöllur in Akureyri today.
A number of tournament records were set on the second day of competition.
An exciting competition was expected in the women's throw at Þórsvöllur, but Vigdís Jónsdóttir from FH set an Icelandic record in 2014 which lasted until Elísabet Rut Rúnarsdóttir from ÍR improved the record in May last year.
Vigdís won the Icelandic record back earlier this summer and has been in fantasy form lately, but she has tripled the Icelandic record for the remainder of the summer.
Elizabeth Rut has been battling an injury and didn't recover today.
She had only one valid throw of five, threw 25.69 yards, which is far from her best.
Vigdís threw for the longest time today, or 60.08 meters in her last attempt and at the same time set a tournament record.
Her Icelandic record set earlier in July is 62.69 meters, so it was quite a bit away.
Gudrún Karítas Hallgrímsdóttir from ÍR had the second best throw of the day, but she improved her best result with a throw of 50.18 meters.
In the men ́s category, the F.B.I. won Hilmar Örn Jónsson, an Icelandic record holder in a hard throw, a safe victory but he threw 73.84 meters in his last throw and set a tournament record.
His Icelandic record is 75,26 meters.
Guðni Valur Guðnason, an Olympian and Icelandic record holder, won a competition in a ballpark at Þórsvöllur yesterday but today he competed in a round throw which is his main subject.
Guðni Valur's Icelandic record is 65,53 meters, but he threw 59.13 meters today and secured the victory.
Valdimar Hjalti Erlendsson threw the second longest today but he had one valid throw that was enough for the 2nd place, 49.43 meters.
Hafdís Sigurðardóttir, the Icelandic record holder in the long jump, won the long jump competition with excellence, while Hafdís jumped the longest 6.25 meters today, which is almost 40 cm from her Icelandic record.
In the women ́s 200-meter race it was Guðbjörg Jóna Bjarnadóttir who came to the finish line in 24.04 seconds but her Icelandic record in the industry is 23.45 seconds.
Guðbjörg Jóna won two gold medals yesterday, in the 100 meters and in a 4 x 100 meter relay race and then also in a 4 x 400 meter relay race today.
In the men ́s category, Kolbeinn Höður Gunnarsson from FH was first to finish in the 200-meter race in 21.57 seconds, 0.3 seconds ahead of Óliver Máni Samúelsson from Ármann.
Like Guðbjörg Jóna, Kolbeinn Höður won gold in the 100 meters yesterday as well as in the 400-meter race.
Doesn't get a fine of 27 million kroner Bitcoin-harvest
The Internal Revenue Board has rejected a claim by a tax investigator who demanded a fine over a man for underestimating his capital gains arising from the sale of the cryptocurrency Bitcoin.
The committee concludes that the Director of Tax Investigations did not make clear why it was necessary to fine the person.
According to the committee's ruling, the Director of Tax Investigations considered that the person had submitted materially incorrect tax returns for the income years in 2016 and 2017.
He would have underestimated his capital gains resulting from the sale of the cryptocurrency Bitcoin of 27 million, either intentionally or out of exquisite negligence.
Guilty carried the man for his conduct.
The man declined in a letter to the committee.
However, he agreed to have sold cryptocurrencies for 27 million in 2016.
She would have had a burial in 2009 and 2010 when it would have been both easy and unnoticed with a normal home computer.
Then he pointed out that when he sold the cryptocurrency, the tax execution for such a sale had been significantly vague and unpredictable.
It could hardly have been expected that ordinary citizens would be aware of such measures on tax returns.
He would not have intended to avoid paying tax on the harvest and would have accounted for his assets in a tax return as a deposit in a foreign exchange account.
He would have inquired from both connoisseurs and the Internal Revenuer about how this would be done but to no avail.
He also believed that it had to be taken into account that he was neither born nor raised in Iceland and had only lived here for a few years when he started digging for Bitcoin.
He would therefore have been in a worse position than others to familiarize himself with complex rules that the tax authorities would not have formed a clear opinion on.
The man therefore considered it appropriate that his taxes for these two income years would be re-assessed and the surcharge applied, but in other respects would not be punished.
He also reiterated that he worked in Iceland and had paid taxes in Iceland since 2012.
He would never have intended to escape his duties unduely.
The demand for a fine would be out of all moderation and would plunge it into debt.
