“Societies in Denmark have probably experienced more of the economic impact of the virus than in Iceland.”
Bicycle whisperer Bjartmar has reclaimed bikes for millions - now responds individually after DV's coverage
For over a year, Bjartmar Leósson has had a hobby, unlike most others.
He snifts up and rescues lost and stolen bicycles, electric bikes and scooters.
Bjartmar has earned the name “wheel whisperer” as a result.
Yesterday there was quite a bit of noise when one published an account of his dealings with Bjartmar.
DV then referred them to a discussion on a Facebook group of Vesturbængar where it was stated that Bjartmar took the man for a speech in Austurvöllur and said the scooter may have been stolen.
There was nothing else to be read from the original writings of the electric shuttle but that Bjartmar thieved him.
That text has now been edited on Facebook and the headline of DV's original news has been updated accordingly.
Bjartmar tells the original news of the case at all not descriptive of what really happened yesterday in Austurvöllur.
Bjartmar said he had received information from the victim of an electric shuttle that this was truly his electric shuttle.
That information got the alleged owner from others, but the information turned out to be incorrect says Bjartmar.
“I saw the guy and I recognized the shuttle, providing information that there was an electric shuttle that had been missing for a very long time.
I certainly hesitated at first, but when I saw him getting ready to drive off on the shuttle, I decide to punch the talk with the man.
The owner was certain of his fault, so I decided to have a conversation with the man.
“In general, I’m very good at things like this, but before I could finish what I had to say, the man had taken the word away from me.”
Bjartmar says that the man on the e-shut immediately invited him to show him the receipt for the bike and called the police himself.
“Yeah, that’s great,” he said, “let’s get this straight.”
It turned out that the owner of the electric shuttle proved ownership of his electric shuttle and drove off.
Later, the proper owner of the electric shuttle told his story on Facebook, as was reported in a previous story.
In just over a year that Bjartmar has practiced this practice, he says that he can count the exchanges he encountered with people on the fingers of one hand.
“I’ve had peaceful relations with Reykjavik’s most difficult men,” Bjartmar said, pointing out that bicycle thieves are by far the smallest of the brothers and sisters of society, addicts, mentally disabled people and other people who for some reason are on the streets.
“Addiction is a tough master and somehow you have to finance the next dose, unfortunately the theft of such movables is an easy path to that goal,” says Bjartmar.
"My communication with these people is actually so good that I've got a lot of good people with me.
"There have been instances of people going to therapy and getting involved, and then reaching out to me and helping me with what I'm doing," he said.
In general, Bjartmar's communication with bikers is on a polite note.
Some people know about him and what he's doing and offered peacefully to show him receipts, trailer numbers and so on.
Bicycle theft is a big problem that little has been addressed.
Furthermore, Bjartmar says that the police are even starting to suggest people to talk to him about stolen bicycles.
Bjartmar disagrees with DV's previous news coverage and says he's not some kind of self-appointed police in a personal search for justice.
Asked if he has not yet come on slippery ice with his actions, and whether this is not first and foremost the role of the police, Bjartmar says it certainly is.
"Of course the cops are supposed to do this, but the fact of the matter is that the cops simply aren't doing it.
For example, I've watched police officers drive away from a large heap of stolen wheels.
“She’s honest about these things.”
“When the cops aren’t doing anything about it, and it’s just in front of your nose, and the experience has shown that I can be successful in this case, then why not?” asks Bjartmar.
He says that he has made such great progress in picking up stolen bikes, that sometimes he bumps into his “clients” on the street that they only had the right Bjartmari bikes that they had previously recovered from.
The time Bjartmar has called for the assistance of the police has happened that she simply does not show up.
"It's just not working with the police, and it's not my fault and it's not the owners of the bicycles to blame," Bjartmar said, and it's sad that the victims of bicycle thefts have to suffer the indifference of the police on this issue.
Bjartmar's performance is not shared.
In many places you can find stories of people thanking Bjartmar for having gotten their belongings back to them.
Bjartmar himself says he has long since lost the number of bicycles he has returned, but the amounts probably run into millions, if not millions.
Weigh electric scooters, e-bikes and electric shuttles heavily, but electric bikes can cost up to half a million.
Bjartmar works during the day in kindergarten and every other weekend he works in cohabitation.
His bicycle search is therefore an unpaid job he performs in his spare time.
Pension funds and long shadows
The year 2019 was a huge anniversary year in the history of pension funds in Iceland.
However, officials had received pensions from the Danish king ever since the 19th century, but in 1919 a pension fund was established that eventually became the pension fund of all civil servants.
The basis for the current pension funds of general wage earners was then laid by collective wage agreements in the labour market in 1969, which provided for employment-related pension funds with compulsory membership and full fund collection from the beginning of 1970.
In 1974, laws were enacted on the basis of these agreements, and the pension system continued to intensify thereafter.
The pension system of wage earners was not the only drastic change that the labour movement of the 20th century propelled with its wage struggle.
Unemployment insurance had been received similarly in historic strikes in 1955, and the association also gradually received its demands for sickness rights and sickness funds, substantial holiday entitlements, shortening of working hours, improvements in housing, and other matters that were of great importance.
All of these rights cost a great and rigorous struggle, but it turned out, in hindsight, a much greater and more permanent benefit than the proliferation in the wage envelope, which disappeared as harshly in inflation, which characterized the period after the war and until 1990, as many people remember.
The lead-up to the establishment of private pension funds in 1969 was both long and complicated.
Even though people eventually agreed that the funds would actually be the property of fund members, the trade union movement had to agree that their boards should appoint representatives of employers and fund members on an equal basis.
In the years following the establishment of the funds, the demand for the majority of workers on the board of directors of the funds was often discussed in the employers' associations, but it never succeeded, and therefore we are still left with the unreasonable arrangement that representatives of fund members are not in the majority on the boards of the funds.
Article 36 of Act No. 129/1997 on Compulsory Insurance of Pension Rights and the Activities of Pension Funds discusses the investment policy of the funds.
Item 1 states that “the pension fund shall be guided by the interests of fund members.”
Item 5 also states: “A pension fund shall set ethical standards for investments.”
The shareholder policy of the Merchants' Pension Fund contains, among other things, the following provisions, following the aforementioned legal provisions:
The Pension Fund is a signatory to the United Nations (UN PRI) rules on responsible investments, as well as some of the largest pension funds and institutional investors in the West and in Europe.
The rules discuss how emphasis on environmental and societal issues as well as good corporate governance can contribute to improving the investment performance of portfolios.
In this way, the interests of investors and the objectives of society are aligned in a broader context.
LV considers it important that companies, especially those listed on the stock market, adopt a public policy on: to maintain good corporate governance for corporate social responsibility and the environment.
The Gildi Pension Fund, which is one of the largest funds in Iceland, has adopted a “policy on accountable investments” and contains provisions of a similar nature.
This is all recalled here as an introduction to the latest fashion word of Icelandic, “shadow management”.
Since the word is new in the case, there is no expectation that it has been fully defined, but it seems to me that the following definition now seems to be “most taken”: Shadow management is when the leader of a trade union says in the media that he intends to send the union’s representatives to the board of the pension fund a recommendation or instructions on how to take a position in a particular case.
If they do not comply with the recommendations, they will be removed from the board at the earliest convenience.
If the leader doesn’t take this to the media and doesn’t talk about dismissal, it’s not “shadow management,” as this kind of communication between people is, of course, always in the financial world as elsewhere.
I'll leave it to the reader to think about which way he'll find "shadier."
There are, in particular, two people who have tried to formulate this definition at the time of writing, on the morning of Saturday, July 25, 2020.
Hörður Ægisson is a journalist at Fréttablaðið and Ásgeir Jónsson, Governor of the Central Bank of Iceland.
The occasion for both came about after Bogi Nils Bogason, CEO of Icelandair, had announced his intention to terminate the company's agreements with flight attendants and flight attendants (tell them), and Ragnar Þór Ingólfsson, chairman of the Reykjavík Chamber of Commerce, sent out a recommendation to the company's representatives on the board of the Traders' Pension Fund not to support the fund's potential purchase of shares in the company.
Some time later Bogi Nils withdrew his decision and subsequently Ragnar Þór took the same route with his recommendations, which were of course based on Bogi's decision.
Nevertheless, Hörður and Ásgeir saw a reason to comment on the matter in the Friday issue of Fréttablaðið with big words about shadow control, violations of the law and a strong need for changes to the law.
There was certainly a small thumper wondering about heavy loads, even though the blessed tuff had been in a subjunctive manner and was also quickly wiped out.
Since it's important, let's think about the material itself at the end.
I mentioned above the points in the Act on Pension Funds pertaining to this matter.
This means that fund boards are obliged to protect the interests of fund members and have ethical criteria in investments, and these issues are elaborated in more detail in the articles of association of both Gildi and the Traders' Pension Fund, as I mentioned.
However, pension funds should NOT look only to short-term profitability considerations in their investments.
This would be a very dangerous policy, and you don't have to look for examples of this in the past, as large loans of banks and funds have flowed to unethical adventurers and environmental disasters, with disastrous consequences.
Icelandair's CEO was obviously on slippery ice when he thought to erase an entire group of employees, thereby significantly reducing the goodwill that the company has enjoyed on the Icelandic market and weakening its position towards investors.
Fortunately, he saw and pulled it back.
Time reveals whether it is sufficient to ensure that pension funds' investments in the company can be considered accountable to fund members in the end.
The author is a former professor of physics and scientific history.
One of the infected did not use homecoming precautions
One of the three people diagnosed with Covid-19) arrived in the country from the Baltic state on 15 July, about a week and a half ago.
However, he/she is an Icelander who does not reside here on a regular basis and therefore did not receive clear instructions to use the so-called repatriation precautions after arriving in Iceland and take another test a few days after arrival.
He got a negative from a screening at the border.
“But he’s in Icelandic society and he’s actually supposed to go to that resource,” Kamilla Jósepsdóttir, a specialist at the Department of Epidemiology at the Directorate of Health and deputy epidemiologist while he’s on summer vacation, told Fréttablaðið today.
“He used the English registration form and it is not obligatory to register the social security number and if the social security number is not registered, you are not automatically summoned for the second sample.”
She says that the man was able to use the English registration form because he actually resides abroad, but because of his network in the country as an Icelander he should have used the Icelandic one.
He didn't realize it, though.
“Once the ID number is not registered, you need to apply yourself for the second sampling.
Neither he nor his employer seems to have realised it was the right way," Kamilla said.
“So it’s clear that we need to strengthen the disclosure of information on this while we find some way to make an automatic preaching system even though Icelanders don’t register their social security numbers.
Or to somehow make it more obvious who are participants in Icelandic society in the registration system,” she explains.
Thankfully, however, only a few of them came to Iceland, even though they are defined as a participant in Icelandic society.
Only six people have been sent into quarantine after he was diagnosed yesterday and these six were all in contact with him.
They have yet to be tested but two of them have begun to show symptoms of Covid-19 infection.
The man received a negative from his sampling at the border on 15 July.
Kamilla says it is likely that he was so recently infected with the virus that she has not yet been able to be detected in him when the sample was taken at the border.
However, she does not want to rule out that the man has been infected here in Iceland but did not carry the virus with him to Iceland.
“It can’t quite be argued that this is indeed an imported infection because it’s been so long ago that he came to the country that he could have been exposed here like those two who have caused a little bit of support in relation to the sports tournaments.”
However, she says it's highly unlikely because of how few people are around here.
“It’s actually very unlikely,” says Kamilla, adding that it can’t be ruled out until deCODE genetics has sequenced the human virus.
"If we get a virus type that hasn't been seen here before, it's almost certain that he carried it to the country."
Pepper sprouts and bombs deployed against protesters
There's fighting between police and protesters in Seattle last night.
Police deployed protesters of pepper sprouts and non-fatal grenades, while protesters broke windows and set on fire.
45 protesters were arrested and 21 officers injured.
Police violence and racism were challenged widely in the United States last night, but the protests in Seattle were in support of protesters in Portland, Oregon.
In Austin, Texas, one protester was shot dead.
