65,000 infections per day
65,490 new coronavirus infections were detected in the U.S. yesterday according to John Hopkins University.
A total of 4,178,021 infections have been confirmed in the West since the beginning of the pandemic.
900 people died from the virus yesterday, but in the last four days up until that, deaths due to the virus were more than 1,000 per day.
A total of 146,460 deaths due to the virus have been confirmed in the United States.
According to the U.S. Chief Epidemiologism, the number of deaths caused by the virus will reach 175,000 on 15 August.
Fearing the second wave of the pandemic
Spain's government is now trying weakly to contain the increased spread of COVID—19 in the country.
Spanish health authorities reported over 920 new cases of COVID—19, both on Thursday and Friday.
There have been no more people diagnosed in one day since the beginning of May and the news arrives at the same time when the Spaniards begin to lift one of Europe's strictest curfews.
The setback led to British authorities now obliging passengers arriving from Spain to quarantine upon returning home.
A week earlier, Spain was on Britain's list of safe countries.
The situation is at its worst in Catalonia in northeastern Spain, where the authorities have taken wider restrictions to try to stem the growth.
In the capital Barcelona, entertainment venues will be closed for the next two weeks and wave houses will be closed at midnight.
A curfew is now in effect for 200,000 residents of Segria county in western Catalonia.
It is believed that Britain's decision will have a negative impact on the Spanish economy which relies heavily on the arrival of foreign tourists and is poorly matched after the pandemic.
Tui, Britain's largest travel agency, cancelled all flights planned today to Spain and the Canary Islands.
Governments around the world are said to be preparing for the second wave of the pandemic, but there seems to be little interest in resorting to an extensive curfew, which has severely devastated the economy.
For example, British Prime Minister Boris Johnson has dealt very poorly with this and likened it to a nuclear weapon he does not want to use.
French Prime Minister Jean Castex has said the nation "would not survive, economically or socially," in the event of a nationwide curfew.
Many decision-makers are hoping for local resources that reach the residents of individual towns, cities or regions will be enough to contain the virus in the next round if it occurs.
Five new infections in Iceland - Three domestic
Three domestic infections were detected yesterday in addition to two in addition at the border.
Thus, five individuals were diagnosed with positive Covid-19 infection yesterday in Iceland.
According to the Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management, one of the infections is related to the infection reported yesterday at the ReyCUP football tournament.
Have they been sent into isolation and sixteen who were in close contact with him in quarantine.
The infected person was a participant in the social activities of a sports club in Reykjavík and according to the notification, "only part of the sports team" is quarantined.
Others who were sent into quarantine relate to the man in a different way.
The origin of this infection is unfounded and infection tracking is in full swing with the National Commissioner's infection tracking team.
The organisers of the ReyCUP tournament have complied with the guidelines and rules of the Chief Epidemiologist and the Department of Civil Protections that are still in force and the appropriate measures have been taken.
However, yesterday's pictures of the tournament attracted attention, as it could be seen in a very close communion celebrating the good luck of his team on the field.
Just before 11 a.m. today, the organisers posted the following announcement on their Facebook page.
“Parenters please observe the rules that contestants pick up their stuff themselves at the school.
Parents are not allowed to do so.
“Thank you for the understanding.”
Another infection was detected yesterday but is related to what was reported the previous day.
According to the report: “Icelandic genetic analysis has sequenced the infection and a new type of virus that has not been detected here has been identified here before.”
There is also an infection tracing in that case and the infected person is in isolation and 12 are in quarantine due to the infection.
The third infection in question is from a man who came to Iceland 11 days ago, on 15 July.
He was diagnosed in the southwest corner of the country.
He is now in isolation and six who were in close contact with him in quarantine.
Two of them have already begun to show symptoms of viral transmission.
In addition, two people were diagnosed at the border and are awaiting the outcome of further research, as provided for in border infections.
Finally, the announcement states: The Civil Protection Department of the National Commissioner of the Icelandic Police and the Directorate of Health urge people to exercise caution and take good care of individual infection control measures.
If there is the slightest doubt as to whether there are symptoms of the Covid-19 virus, the same person is asked to go for testing at the nearest clinic.
Patreksfjörður says that the camp site is booked during the shopping weekend - Directs guests another
The camp site in Patreksfjörður is fully booked this weekend, according to Vesturbyggð's announcement.
Visitors are advised to visit other accommodations.
Mentions Vesturbyggð Bíldudalur, Tálknafjörður, Melanes á Rauðasandur, Hotel Flókalundur and Hotel Breiðavík as other options in the situation.
There is plenty going on in Patreksfjörður this weekend, because as in previous years, Skjaldborg, a festival of Icelandic documentaries will be held in the town.
It has been held since 2007 and has therefore become a strong tradition in the town.
Judging from the demand for the campsite, one can expect good attendance this year, if the weather does not put a mark in the account, but DV said earlier today about a poor forecast.
However, one can expect the worst weather in the south, and hope for the Patreksfjörður residents.
The website bb.is first reported, and said that all the other accommodation in the town was already fully booked.
An entire condominium decomposes to the best place in the city
Romanian workers live for free at Dunhagi 18–20.
In the meantime, the owners are looking for a building permit for renovations, but their errand has been successful in the system for the third year.
In one of the oldest, vegetated and most expensive districts of the City of Reykjavík stands a magnificent multi-family building on three floors.
On the ground floor of the building there is about 600 square meters of commercial space.
Behind the house is the door of two stairwells, each of which contains four fairly large apartments.
They range from 93–130 square meters, although mostly over 100.
The house has quite a history.
It was built in 1959 and has, among other things, housed a video rental company, Skóstofan, Mjólkursamsalan's milk shop, Jóa gunsmith, fish sale and most recently Háskólaprent.
The house has, to put it mildly, more beautifully remembered.
As you can see in the pictures below, the house is under a lot of damage.
The owner of the house is D18 ehf.
The owners of D18 ehf. are, according to the register of companies, among others Magnús Magnússon and Guðrún Helga Lárusdóttir.
Magnus was in charge of Borgun's ownership group and was the head of the holding company Borgun.
Among the owners of the holding company Borgun is Stálskip ehf.
Stálskip ehf. is an investment company of Guðrún Helga Lárusdóttir and her children.
Gudrún is also the owner of a third share in D18 ehf.
Gudrún and her husband, Ágúst Gudmundur Sigurdsson, once operated the fishing company Stálskip.
D18 ehf. bought the house in the summer of 2009 and has therefore not been maintained since then.
The neighbors say the condition of the house has deteriorated steadily since, though mostly in recent years.
On the neighborhood's Facebook page, one resident says the house was "not particularly lively in recent years."
It may play into the fact that owners have for several years sought to change the house and the plot.
Other neighbors say the house for a long time has not been okay.
"It's long overdue to do something decent about this spot and it's sad that it dragged on like this."
He also says that he's pissed at the owners of the house for not trying to finish it, found some common surface with the house's neighbors and stopped this "legal stamp."
“Just do it in consultation with the community so you can get it done and finish it.”
“The legal stamp” to which the resident refers is the planning process of the field that has been underway at least since 2017.
The owners of Dunhagi 18 and 20 sought permission to build a floor on top of the existing apartment building and behind the house, a new elevator building and an extension on one floor as well as a basement.
The building permit that was granted for that project was appealed to the Appeals Committee on Environment and Natural Resources, and the committee revoked it as the project had no basis in the zoning plan and the local presentation was not satisfactory.
The City of Reykjavík then started with a zoning project and completed it with an advertisement in the Official Journal in July last year.
That structure was also appealed and the committee repealed it in March 2020.
At this point, nearly three years had passed since the initial application for a building permit and the owners of the building at the beginning.
During that time, the house had dragged down considerably and the neighbors began to get tired.
When DV asked the neighbor of the building about the situation and the reaction of the neighbors, the answers were in various ways.
Some had an understanding of the intentions of the owners of the house, others did not at all.
Others turned their anger at the city, others didn't.
Others were just angry, but they weren't into anyone.
Still others said the parking lots were disputed, but neighbours have taken advantage of Dunhagi's unused parking space 18-20 under their vehicles.
One complainant in the case said he was tired of the administration:
“Let’s have to go through the process three times is unbelievable.
"It's as if the City of Reykjavík couldn't read."
Today, the house stands empty, abandoned and is neglected.
A memorial to the city's recent administration and the noble intentions of the owner and their years of work that is now at the beginning.
When a journalist arrived at Dunhagi 18, he was confronted with galley doors and piles of rubbish.
Playstating computer and recent television lay among other debris on the planet — victims of the Icelandic summer wet.
It was opened into the old office of Háskólaprent, where quite a few cats of the pee smell to judge.
It's clear that someone's nestled in one corner, but nobody's visible.
Stacks of sofas and beds and a few pallets of geological pages, which no doubt were supposed to turn into a book.
The stairwells of the apartment building were also open and life in both of them.
A journalist was lucky enough to run into the inhabitants of one of them.
The Romanians were boiling a potato dish for dinner and invited a journalist in.
The Romanians work for the temporary-work agency Ztrongforce ehf.
They have been there for some times and DV's sources simulate that the company paid nothing for the premises except heating and electricity.
Due to the condition of the house, it is not considered defensible to collect rent.
Judging by the circumstances of a mail in the lobby of the building, it is clear that there have been a large number of foreign workers living there in the recent past.
Temporary-to-human management has not been part of the Covid-19 situation, as the contraction in tourism has led to a contraction in the construction sector, and these two industries have been most efficiously taking advantage of Temporary agency services.
Nevertheless, the household members at Dunhagi 18 seemed to have plenty to do and hung the working suits and mittens in the common area to dry after a long working day in the rain.
It should be noted that despite the miserable condition of the house, the apartment that the boys shared seemed to be well maintained.
When a reporter said goodbye to the Romanian boys, the irony suddenly made him clear: At the best place in Reykjavík, 1,500 square meters of real estate stands down.
The owners want to change the house for the better and the neighbors want an improved situation but argue about the definition of “recovery.”
In between, the city's planning department sits at the beginning, the victim of endless appeals and appeals in the planning process, and the final resolution of the Appeals Committee on Environment and Natural Resources.
In the house there are Romanian workers, perhaps precisely those who go into improving the situation, when the Icelanders stop arguing.
The article originally appeared in DV's weekend paper on July 17.
In the campaign against overweight in the wake of the coronavirus pandemic
Britain's prime minister, Boris Johnson, will provide 10 million pounds in a campaign against overweight, which will include a ban on fast-food advertisements, as a result of him getting seriously ill, partly because of his weight.
It is planned that Johnson will present the campaign, which has been named Better Health, tomorrow, Monday, but with the campaign, doctors will be encouraged to prescribe cycling for their overweight clients and an effort to increase the number of cycling paths.
Advertisements of fast food venues on television will then be banned before 21 o'clock in the evening, according to British media sources.
“COVID—19 has provoked us from overweight short and long-term risks, and the prime minister is determined that we must use the time to become healthier, more active, and eat healthier diets,” a government spokesman said.
Johnson himself has had a weight problem, but he was admitted to intensive care when he became infected with the coronavirus in the spring, which is partly due to which he is believed to have fallen ill so severely.
Women better suited to leading the changes
She said the new constitution is the biggest step that the nation can take towards greater decentralisation, transparency and in working against corruption and the interests of the whole.
“We need to change gears a little bit right now, and who’s better suited to lead that kind of change rather than the group of communities that find it easier to be in those values.
"It's women," Helga said.
"Women in Iceland are famous for their women's solidarity.
To stand together and be united in leading changes.
“It’s time for us to make these changes.”
She said that she was a little worried about the emphasis on Iceland as "the best in the world" in both gender equality and human rights.
“We’re just as excited to see what we have to do.
As the Germans are very aware of their history and look at everything that needs to be done.
There's lots.
We have to know where we come from and know the story and listen.
Listen to the voices of marginalized groups.
"We don't have the same rules here," Helga said.
When asked, she said that women are in many respects in good shape in Iceland, but added that, as in many other places, Icelanders have very masculine values.
She said that politics revolved around, for example, its own interests, dominance, and power, which were the forces that maintained inequalities in all societies.
“Women have now set up this group and come together and are just getting a bit annoyed that Althingi intends to overlook this referendum from 2012,” she said.
"It's these feminine values that we're thinking about.
Based on human rights and nature conservation, co-operation, and that we all really sit at the same table," she said, adding that the aforementioned values were the core values of the new constitution.
“We’re a very rich country of natural resources, and it’s absurd that there are people living in poverty.
"It's ridiculous, we can switch differently," Helga said.
"It's absurd that it's a natural law that women's jobs are always paid.
"It's absurd that flight attendants and nurses will have to fight collective bargaining endlessly just in order to get a decent wage."
It is possible to sign the petition for a new constitution here through electronic Iceland.
