A man was arrested in the city centre last night after he threatened pedestrians and tried to get money from him.
The police also stopped the production of drugs in Árbær where two people were detained.
The police also stopped the driver of a motorcycle in Hlíðar where its driver was at a speed of 146 km with a maximum speed of 60 km/h.
Moreover, he had previously been deprived of a driving licence.
The Metropolitan Police had enough going on last night and more than 80 cases were recorded in the police diary from five yesterday until five this morning.
There were a lot of noise reports because of parties.
On the night before Saturday, there were reports of eleven noisy parties, and this was widely seen in the police diary.
Last night, however, 22 noise issues were found on police's table.
Six people were kept in a prison cell last night.
In Árbær, police were summoned last night when parties launched fireworks.
However, they had escaped when officers arrived.
Then, nine drivers were stopped for intoxication and or drug driving.
Four people were arrested over a fight in the city centre last night, one of whom was imprisoned.
Then two people were taken to the emergency room after they fell on their faces, one in the city centre and the other in the West.
One was taken to the emergency room after he was injured while jumping on a trampoline in Kópavogur.
In addition, police officers stopped the driver who pulled a caravan in Kjalarnes yesterday.
The caravan's bicycle equipment was, according to the police's diary, in a "very bad condition" and it was stationary.
You can stay in Monet's house during the shopping weekend
The house, in which Impressionist Claude Monet spent the last forty years of his life, is now for rent on the Airbnb site.
The next vacant nights in the house are during the shopping weekend.
The house is small and adorable, located in the town of Givenry in Normandie, France.
The world-famous painter lived in the house from 1883 until his death in 1926.
There are three bedrooms, two lounges and three bathrooms.
Monet was first inspired to paint his gardens famous in this house.
If someone intends to rent the house, they must rent at least two nights.
Considering Airbnb's page, it's almost vacant in a week, on the Sunday shopping weekend, so it might be ideal for some shopkeepers on vacation to hurry to book the painter's house.
The two nights over the shopping weekend cost only US$964 or just over 130 thousand Icelandic krónur as far as Fréttablaðið can get.
Dozens in quarantine and several of them beginning to show symptoms of the disease caused by the coronavirus
Five people have been diagnosed with the coronavirus domestically in the last three days and most of the infected are unrelated.
Dozens have been quarantined because of this and several of them have begun to show symptoms of the disease caused by the virus.
Three people were diagnosed with the coronavirus domestically yesterday and two on border screening.
An expert at the Directorate of Health says that the increasing number of domestic infections does not necessarily mean that the virus is spreading widely to society.
“There has been a sequencing from the previous domestic infection and there is a virus that has not been seen here before, so we have no particular reason to believe that it has been hidden in the community for some time.
This is probably something that has just arrived in the country, but of course we need to be very wary right now,” said Kamilla Sigríður Jósefsdóttir, an expert in the Department of Epidemiology at the Directorate of Health.
Most of those infected have been associated with individuals who came from abroad.
Further testing will be carried out in the near future, but a few, who were in contact with those infected, are beginning to show symptoms of the coronavirus.
“Right now there’s no influenza going on and less about other respiratory infections so we can be pretty liberal by doing these tests on individuals who during an influencing season would have found a reason to do something else first,” Kamilla said.
Infection tracking has mostly been completed, but it is not impossible that more people will have to go into quarantine.
Two of the infected were diagnosed after attending sporting events.
It raises the question of whether it is defensible to hold such events.
The Director of Communications for Civil Protection says that this is possible as long as people follow guidelines and rules.
Kamilla says it's almost impossible that the infected have been infected at the sports tournaments.
“If there are no more people in isolation based on these individuals after being around them at these sports tournaments, then we can argue that our infection prevention in the tournaments has worked.
"But that's not clear," Kamilla said.
The Civil Protection Department's procedures are under constant review.
"We need to be willing to intervene with further advice or restrictions if there seems to be cause," Kamilla said.
In just over a week, mass restrictions are expected to be extended to a thousand people.
This new situation could affect concessions.
“Determined Meditation to Cooke” – See Elisa Viðar’s menu
Elísa Viðarsdóttir is an accomplished footballer and plays with Valur.
She is also a master's student in nutrition, a mother, and works as a food scientist.
It requires a lot of energy for the day's activities and usually takes the time to cook good and nutritious food.
"My normal day starts with coming to work by eight," she said.
"After work, I go to the store to prepare dinner before picking up my girl at the nursery around three o'clock."
Elísa finds it very nutritious to pick up her daughter early in the nursery.
"It's good to have time with her before I go to training later.
After exercise, it's good to come home and just have to warm up the food.
At night, when the girl is asleep, we like to watch one episode to empty her mind.”
Elisha doesn't follow a certain diet.
She is completing a master's thesis in nutrition and therefore knows well what suits her to eat to have enough energy to do work, school, the family and exercises.
“What suits me is to eat a variety of foods that are well formulated with proteins, carbohydrates and fats.
However, I think it's most important to have a healthy relationship with food and don't categorize food into bad or good, rather nutritious or nutritious.
“It’s okay to eat everything and not always.”
Elisha is very interested in cooking.
“I think there’s a lot of thinking about cooking in the kitchen.
"I have to say that my self-confidence is with me in the kitchen, and I truly believe I'm a good cook."
Breakfast: Oats, chia seeds, hemp seeds, salt, a little lemon juice, leave in almond milk overnight.
Sprinkle this meal with what’s available.
Most of the time, it's a banana and crispy muesli & COFFEE.
- I'm a great coffee lady.
Intermediates: Incredibly uneven, but fruits or vegetables, flatbread, pure skyr with banana and muesli, bread with toppings and then I could eat hummus with a spoon out of a box if that is the case.
Lunch: I often prepare all sorts of delicious salads from what is available in the fridge, quinoa or barley, falafel buns, rock lettuce, oven-baked vegetables with a good dressing is a bit what I'm working with.
If the layout goes completely out of control (which often happens) then the egg machine at work has helped me quite often and then there are 2 slices of bread with butter, cheese and boiled egg, don't put in any more.
Intermediaries: Get me something high in carbohydrates for exercise, bread with toppings, cereals or fruit.
Dinner: Fish will very often be chosen in my home, otherwise some delicious vegetarian dishes.
Conflicts between Police and Protesters in Seattle
Seattle city officials have declared an insurgency in the wake of crowded protests in the city centre.
Police yesterday resorted to flare bombs and pepper sprouts to try to pave a large area that protesters conquered and stretched across many blocks in the vicinity of the city's parliament building.
The police announced on Twitter that at least eleven protesters had been arrested and that an investigation was underway into sabotage carried out at a police station in the city yesterday, possibly with some kind of bomb.
City and police officials say protesters stoned police with rocks, bottles, detonators and other loose ones and one officer was taken to a hospital where his injuries were repaired.
However, Seattle's protests were peaceful for a long time.
They were blown to show solidarity to protesters in Portland, Oregon, but there has repeatedly been fierce clashes between protesters and heavily armededed quorumes by FBI agents.
There, as in other U.S. cities, people gather under the banner of the Black Lives Matter, remembered Georges Floyds, who was killed by a Minneapolis police officer on May 25, systematically protests racism in U.S. society and demands improvements.
Not revoked until after voting
It is not yet clear whether Icelandair's flight attendant's dismissals, which are due to take effect at the end of next month, will be revoked.
An electronic vote on the collective wage agreement of the Icelandic Stewardess Association on behalf of Icelandair's flight attendants ends at noon tomorrow.
Guðlaug Líney Jóhannsdóttir, the company's chairman, says that she is confident that Icelandair will not make a decision on the layoffs until the result of the vote has been announced.
"It's definitely hanging together," says Guðlaug Líney.
"There have been no redundancies, this has to happen so that the machines can be manned.
“Of course, people are impatient to know if they’ll have a job after the end of the month.”
An agreement that was signed a week ago will be voted on on the night of 19 July.
It is expected to apply until the end of September 2025 and is based on an agreement that flight attendants had previously voted on.
On 17 July, the Board of Directors and the Consultative Council of the Icelandic Stewardess' Association agreed to call a full-time work stoppage for Icelandair. Members agreed to do so in a vote.
It never came to it, but this was decided in the wake of Icelandair's decision to cease negotiations with the airline, terminate all its flight attendants and seek agreements with another union.
Then the company's pilots were supposed to take on the work of security personnel on board temporarily.
Asked how she thinks the vote will go, Guðlaug Líney says it's difficult to say.
“People are hurting after Icelandair, when all the company’s flight attendants were terminated and announced that another union would be negotiated.
"It's now clear if it's having an impact," she said.
940 flight attendants worked for Icelandair at the end of April, when 900 of them, about 95%, were terminated.
Their notice period varies, for those who have the shortest working life when it is three months and ends at the beginning of July-August.
This group comprises about 90% of Icelandair flight attendants.
What happens if the deal isn’t approved?
"We are looking for continued discussions with our counterparties.
With this agreement, we are meeting Icelandair's requirements.
If it is rejected, it is clear that flight attendants have gone too far.”
Voting on the collective wage agreement concludes and Icelandair announces the results for the second quarter tomorrow
Flight attendant's vote on a new collective agreement ends at 12 noon tomorrow.
Icelandair's results for the second quarter will also be published tomorrow, but preliminary operating results indicate that the company's revenue has declined by 85 percent from the same period last year.
An electronic vote on a collective agreement between the Icelandic Stewardess Association and the Confederation of Icelandic Employers (SA) began on Wednesday, 22 July, and conclude tomorrow, Monday 27 July, at 12 noon.
Those who are allowed to vote on the agreement are employees of Icelandair who pay membership fees to the airline.
Icelandair and FFÍ signed a new collective agreement on the night of Sunday 19 July. On Friday, Icelandair had terminated negotiations with the company after flight attendants struck a previous agreement in a referendum at the beginning of July.
Furthermore, Icelandair had terminated all flight attendants and flight attendances on Friday, but they were revoked after a new collective wage agreement was signed.
According to the new collective agreement, flight attendants have to fly five more hours a month for the same basic salary.
The collective wage agreement is valid until 2025, where the provisions of pilots and flight attendants have been integrated on how long it is possible to fly on a single shift.
The agreement was presented to FFÍ's members at a meeting at the Hilton Nordica hotel last Monday and many flight attendants who were interviewed by the news agency expressed dissatisfaction about the agreement.
Most, however, seemed to agree, however, that it would have to accept the deal in order to keep FFÍ alive.
Icelandair's results for the second quarter will be announced tomorrow, but the company's EBIT, its operating results for financial items and taxes, is negative by US$100 to US$110 million or about US$15 billion.
Icelandair sent a preliminary Calculation last Wednesday, stating that the company's revenue amounted to US$60 million in the quarter, or about 8.3 billion ISK.
The cash equivalent was US$154 million at the end of the quarter, around US$21 billion.
Icelandair also plans to offer the company's shares in August.
Icelandair plans to finalise agreements with fifteen creditors, the government and the Boeing aircraft manufacturer by the end of the month before an auction will be held.
A new variant rather than a new type of virus
"A new virus just means that it's been an individual who came from abroad.
This is not something that has been simmering here in Iceland,” says Már Kristjánsson, chief medical officer at the infectious diseases department of Landspítali University Hospital, about the news that “a new type of virus” has been detected in Iceland.
Three domestic infections were detected yesterday and two at the border.
One who had been diagnosed was involved in social activities of a sports club at the Rey Cup, but the source of the infection is unfounded and infection tracing is ongoing.
An infection was also diagnosed in a person who arrived in the country on 15 July and two who were in contact with him started to show symptoms of COVID—19.
An infection was also detected yesterday related to the infection reported the previous day and after sequencing at deCODE genetics it was revealed “a new type of virus that has not been detected here before.”
Infection tracking is completed in connection with that infection.
Már points out that there is not a new virus, but that this is the same virus that has spread around the world, i.e. the new coronavirus SARS-CoV-2.
It's more correct to talk about a new variant of that virus than about a new type of virus.
Asked what it means and whether it could say anything about the start of another wave of epidemics, Már says that it is currently a single case and it could happen that there will be nothing more out of it.
“On the other hand, if there are cases in Iceland that do not have links from abroad and have the same genotype as this particular variant, it would be possible to draw that conclusion [about the second wave], but it is premature at this time,” explains Már.
Happiness in the hot tub
Is there a secret to Iceland's happiness hidden in the hot tubs?
The BBC's programme-makers lead to the likes of an entertaining video that sheds light on the country's pool and hot tub culture.
The Icelandic bath culture is unique worldwide and it is stated that there are nowhere in the world there are so many bathing spots per capita.
The natural geothermal energy is its foundation, but also the tradition of swimming here.
Children's swimming lessons were mandatory in 1940, but senior citizens are no less efficient in using the hot water for their health.
Swimmers are described as a fixed part of the country's general well-being.
In the pot, everyone is equal, regardless of class and status.
Free of mobile phones, people talk together on a personal note at home and abroad or enjoy the palliable power of the water: meditates and recharges the batteries of the soul and body.
On the steep to pick up a hundred days of elections
There are hundreds of days until the United States goes to the polls and chooses a four-year president.
Donald Trump seeks re-election, but he's supposed to be picking up.
Joe Biden, the forthcoming candidate of the Democratic Party, has a huge advantage over Trump at the national level, taking into account opinion polls.
According to a new survey by the AP, a record number of Americans believe the nation is on the wrong track.
Trump's response to the coronavirus pandemic is also highly unpopular, and in addition, more Americans than before believe that the president has detained the economy.
More specifically, only two out of ten Americans say the U.S. is on the right track.
32 percent say they support Trump's response to the pandemic, and 48 percent say he's held well on economic issues.
In March it was 56 percent and in January it was 67 percent.
According to FiveThirtyEight's average, Biden's adherency is 49.9 percent nationwide, while Trump's support is 41.9 percent.
Trump himself has tried to shift attention away from his performances towards the pandemic to Biden, promoting so-called cultural disputes and proclaiming policies that are supposed to relate to law and order.
Biden's candidacy, however, makes every effort to keep his focus on Trump and considers it highly likely to win if the election really revolves around how Trump has done his job in the last four years.
Trump's unpopularity also appears to be coming down on Republican senators, fearing the Democrats could even gain a majority in the U.S. Senate, which so far has been highly unlikely.
Politico recently announced that if the parties and the candidate's support remains comparable as it is now, the Republican Party would receive its biggest outrage in decades.
Suburbs have proved to be particularly bad for the party in the 2016 parliamentary elections, and it now appears that this trend will continue.
In recent days, Trump has tried to inspire suburban residents to accompany him, among other things, by saying that if President Biden becomes president, he will destroy U.S. suburbs and promote racial controversy.
Among other things, Trump has suspended regulation since Barack Obama's time in the White House designed to diversify the suburbs.
Then he urged “housewives” of U.S. suburbs to read an article by former deputy governor of New York, claiming Biden would erase U.S. suburbs and Trump agreed.
"Biden will destroy your neighborhoods and your American dream.
“I’ll preserve it, and I’ll make it even better,” he said.
The population of U.S. suburbs is an ever-growing group of voters.
According to NPR, they make up about half of all voters in the United States.
Ever since George W. Bush was re-elected in 2004, the candidate who has received the majority of the votes of this group has become president.
Except in 2012 when Mitt Romney received a majority of votes from this group but lost to Barack Obama.
Opinion polls have shown that even though Trump secures a small majority in the 2016 suburbs, his support has declined sharply.
Although it varies between polls, Biden has measured a fifteen percentage point advantage over Trump in the outskirts recently.
Regis Philbin has died
U.S. broadcaster Regis Philbin has died at the age of 88.
Philbin worked as an actor, host, promoter and singer for about six decades.
He is best known for running the popular talk show Live! with Regis from 1988 to 2011 along with Kathie Lee Gifford and later Kelly Ripa.
He has also directed Who Wants to Be a Millionaire and America's Got Talent.
According to Guinness World Records, Philbin is the person who has spent most hours on American television and has been recognized to that effect.
He received six Emmy Awards during his working life and was nominated 37 times in total.
According to a statement from his family, Philbin died of natural causes.
A number of colleagues, friends and fans have remembered him on social media over the last 24 hours.
Will the Olympic fire be the light at the end of the tunnel?
After the final decision had been made to postpone the Olympics, an event that only world wars has plagued so far, the president of the International Olympic Committee said that the famous Olympic fire would be "the light at the end of the tunnel," presumably referring to the coronavirus pandemic that the world is now going through together.
Achievement workers have years to prepare, and authorities in Japan improve on costs due to the delay.
However, everyone maintains a header as much at stake.
When it was announced that Tokyo in Japan would be the venue for this year's Olympics, the Japanese delegates' celebrations were genuine.
They wept and laughed alternately, as Tokyo had applied to host the Games in 2016, but it bowed that time to a lower level for Rio in Brazil.
This time, Tokyo City and Japan should bathe in the world's attention.
The Games are scheduled to take place from 24 July to 9 August but have been postponed for a year and will begin instead on 23 July 2021 and end on 8 August.
In the event of failing to hold the Games, if the coronavirus will still be too much of a threat, the Games will be canceled.
However, no one really wants to think that way to the end.
The Olympics are no ordinary sports competition.
There's nothing to spare, it's always supposed to be spectacular and strives to top the last play.
The choice of a city for the Olympic Games is based on the selection committee's assessment of the promotions of the cities.
The more spectacular the presentation—the greater the likelihood of receiving the prize.
Tokyo spent 150 million dollars trying to host the 2016 Olympics, or just over US$20 billion.
For the second time, when applying for the 2020 Games, US$75 million, about ten billion ISK, was put into the presentation.
In 2013, when it was decided that Tokyo would receive this year's Games, the Japanese authorities had already spent 30 billion ISK on the project.
But that amount, however, is just a drop in the ocean compared to how much it costs to build Olympic villages, stadiums and in general to strengthen the country's infrastructure in order to handle such tournaments, prepare it and hold the games themselves.
Organizers in Japan have said the Tokyo Olympics, which should have been underway these days but has been postponed for one year because of the coronavirus, would have cost US$12.6 billion.
However, according to a report released at the end of last year, a report from the National Audit Office in Japan stated that the cost was nearly double that number.
It will probably not be possible to fully estimate the cost of postponing the Games, but it has been estimated that it can cost between two and six billion dollars in addition to the original costs.
The total cost for the Japanese Olympic Committee and Japanese taxpayers could therefore run between 15 and US$30 billion.
There are such high figures that all the tax revenues of the Icelandic state would only suffice for about half of the Olympics, given the lowest possible costs.
The Olympics are an event of such magnitude that they have often become the subject of economists, who try to examine the numbers and look at the benefits and benefits of the Games.
In short, it seems that quite a number of people have come to the conclusion that the effects of the Olympic Games are less than so positive for the economies of the cities that hold them.
There are some short-term effects, for example the number of jobs is greatly increased in the short term, but in the long term cities tend to be left with debt tails and high operating costs of little used structures.
Rio in Brazil is left with significant debts due to the 2016 Games and has been having trouble costing maintenance on all the big sports facilities built before the Games.
Figures have been reviewed after the 2012 London Games, where it has been revealed that only 10 per cent of those who received jobs related to the Olympic Games in the city were previously unemployed.
This means we didn't talk about new jobs except to a small extent.
In general, cities have not been particularly successful financially hosting the Olympics because of the slugging costs associated with structures built before the Games.
However, the benefits are often thought to consist of increased traffic of tourists who want to visit the Olympic cities following the Games, but there is great uncertainty regarding the 2021 Games.
It is also uncertain whether it will be possible to accept all the number of spectators who usually attend the Games.
However, the main issue is the honor that the cities have chosen, but it is difficult to estimate for money.
Therefore, in spite of the enormous cost, it can also be said that the joy that the games bring is not such that it is possible to put a price tag on it.
But even though the show is often magnificent and there is no saving, of course, it's not just about money.
It's the sports heroes who are in the foreground.
For some accomplished people, it's surprisingly just welcome to postpone the games.
For example, the Australian five-athlete and gold medalist in her article at the last Olympics, Chloe Esposito, is, for example, very light and would have been far from fun this summer, but expects to be in competition form for the 2021 Games.
She is, therefore, among those athletes who are just pretty happy with the postponement, for understandable reasons.
For those athletes who planned to retire after this year's Games, had they been held on time, the postponement of the games in some instances means that they quit the competition before the Games take place.
Trust their bodies simply don't in order to go through years in addition to rigorous workouts.
Few athletes established themselves so thoroughly in the hearts of spectators at the 2016 Rio Olympics and gymnastics star Simone Biles.
She came home with four golds around her neck and one bronze.
Biles has attended several interviews in his U.S. country recently to discuss the Olympics.
She plans to attend the 2021 Games, but she's not sure if she'll still be at the top of the Games next year, as she'll be 24.
Although it is generally not considered an advanced age, it is at a higher level for a top gymnast.
"It's a sensitive issue," Biles said, but smiles in an interview posted on the Olympic Committee's Instagram page when asked if it intends to go as far at the 2021 Games as it did in Rio 2016.
"I honestly don't know if I'll still be on top after another year of training," Biles said.
She has previously said that her body does not tolerate the stress that comes with gymnastics training much longer.
Nevertheless, she trains hard for the 2021 Olympics.
Biles admits it was an uncomfortable feeling to have to stop suddenly exercising when the pandemic was at its peak and the gymnasium was closed.
There were no exceptions for Biles rather than others while all sports facilities were closed for seven weeks.
All her Olympic gold could not buy any access beyond the others, she had to find various ways to stay in shape just like everyone else.
It’s much better than most people.
"We've got a stiff plan right now.
It was difficult to start training first again after the gymnasium opened.
We started slowly but are in full swing again and I will increase the training steadily as the year progresses.
Of course, we don't really know how these games will be or whether they will be held, but we still practice considering they will be, we can't do anything else.
“I’ve put too much effort into leaving the sport right now,” Biles said.
With it, such as others, there is no doubt, or perhaps more awareness that there may arise that the Games will by no means be held in 2021 either.
However, predicting it is hopeless.
No one can know how the situation during the coronavirus pandemic will be in July 2021 and there is nothing else to do but prepare for the Olympics considering they will be held in a year.
Maybe the Olympic fire will be the light at the end of the Covid tunnel.
Postponing weddings due to pandemic
Modern family actress Sarah Hyland has decided to postpone her wedding due to the coronavirus.
She was due to marry former Bachelorette contestant Wells Adams this summer.
"I think there's other more important things to think about right now," the actress told People.
“We really want to get married someday and have a dream wedding and have all the attendees who are dear to us.
But we decided to postpone it and focus on what is important right now and it is helping to share information about the importance of wearing masks and staying at home.
I'm looking more at world affairs than weddings these days.
There's a lot going on and we're supposed to focus our attention on what's going on in the world."
